      {
        "h_title": "Lod chong press alternative",
        "main_title": "Thai Lod Chong Dessert (Cendol Recipe)",
        "author": "Praew",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Plastic bag method : If you don’t have a lod chong press, simply place the cooked pandan dough in a plastic bag, snip off a small corner, and squeeze out the pandan noodles.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Lod chong recipe tips",
        "main_title": "Thai Lod Chong Dessert (Cendol Recipe)",
        "author": "Praew",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Serve chilled : Thai lod chong is best served chilled. It’s perfect for making ahead of time and keeping it in the refrigerator for later.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Sweetness : Feel free to adjust the palm sugar syrup to your taste. You can easily turn this into a less or more sweet dessert.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Pandan : If you can’t find fresh pandan leaves, you can use pandan extract as a substitute. A few drops are typically enough.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Presentation tips",
        "main_title": "Thai Lod Chong Dessert (Cendol Recipe)",
        "author": "Praew",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "You can serve this dessert in a bowl or present it in a tall glass as a lod chong drink. Don’t forget crushed ice and top with your favorite toppings like sweet corn, jackfruit, konjac jelly, and sliced fruits.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Storage and make ahead",
        "main_title": "Thai Lod Chong Dessert (Cendol Recipe)",
        "author": "Praew",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Store your leftovers in an airtight container in your refrigerator. It’ll keep well for up to 3 days. If possible, store the noodles and coconut milk separately.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Frequently asked questions",
        "main_title": "Thai Lod Chong Dessert (Cendol Recipe)",
        "author": "Praew",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "What is cendol made of?",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Cendol is made of a mixture of rice flour or tapioca flour, coconut milk, and pandan leaf extract, creating a green, jelly-like dessert often served with coconut milk and palm sugar syrup.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Is this recipe gluten-free?",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Yes, rice flour and tapioca starch are naturally gluten-free.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "How can I make lod chong green without pandan leaves?",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Use a few drops of pandan extract instead.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
